<DemoExample Title="Prefix & Suffix" RazorCode="@example13RazorCode" Id="example13">
    <div>Wraps the content of each option in plain text: Prefix is rendered at the start of the option label, before the option text, and Suffix after the content of the option. Pairing Suffix with FullWidth and an auto inline start margin turns it into a trailing column, which is where a price or a hint usually belongs. For arbitrary content on either end, use the ItemPrefixTemplate and ItemSuffixTemplate shown in the Item templates section.</div>
    <br />
    <div class="example-content column">
        <BitChoiceGroup Label="Shipping method (Prefix)" DefaultValue="@("Standard")" TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string">
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Standard" Value="@("Standard")" Prefix="$0 - " />
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Express" Value="@("Express")" Prefix="$10 - " />
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Overnight" Value="@("Overnight")" Prefix="$25 - " />
        </BitChoiceGroup>

        <div class="example-box">
            <BitChoiceGroup Label="Shipping method (Suffix)"
                            DefaultValue="@("Standard")"
                            FullWidth
                            Styles="@(new() { ItemSuffix = "margin-inline-start: auto;" })"
                            TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string">
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Standard" Value="@("Standard")" Suffix="Free" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Express" Value="@("Express")" Suffix="$10" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Overnight" Value="@("Overnight")" Suffix="$25" />
            </BitChoiceGroup>
        </div>
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Events" RazorCode="@example14RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example14CsharpCode" Id="example14">
    <div>Raises OnChange with the new value whenever the selection actually changes, and OnClick with the whole option on every activation of an enabled option, including a click on the already selected one and an arrow key press, which the browser also reports as a click. OnFocus and OnBlur fire with the option as the focus moves between the options. All four stay silent while the group is disabled or read-only.</div>
    <br />
    <div class="example-content column">
        <BitChoiceGroup Label="Events" DefaultValue="@("A")" TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string"
                        OnChange="(string? value) => changedValue = value"
                        OnClick="(BitChoiceGroupOption<string> option) => clickedOption = option.Text"
                        OnFocus="(BitChoiceGroupOption<string> option) => focusedOption = option.Text"
                        OnBlur="(BitChoiceGroupOption<string> option) => blurredOption = option.Text">
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option A" Value="@("A")" />
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option B" Value="@("B")" />
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option C" Value="@("C")" />
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option D" Value="@("D")" />
        </BitChoiceGroup>
        <div>Changed value: <b>@changedValue</b></div>
        <div>Clicked option: <b>@clickedOption</b></div>
        <div>Focused option: <b>@focusedOption</b></div>
        <div>Blurred option: <b>@blurredOption</b></div>
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Dynamic items" RazorCode="@example15RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example15CsharpCode" Id="example15">
    <div>Handles dynamic option collections: an option rendered after the first render registers itself at the end of the list, no matter where in the markup it is located, so its assigned Index (shown here as a number prefix) would be out of order; AutoReorderOptions reads the DOM order after each render to keep the indexes in sync with the markup, preserving the selected value as long as its option survives the change.</div>
    <br />
    <div class="example-content column">
        <div>
            <BitButton OnClick="AddDynamicOption">Add item</BitButton>
            <BitButton OnClick="RemoveDynamicOption">Remove item</BitButton>
            <BitButton OnClick="ReverseDynamicOptions">Reverse items</BitButton>
        </div>
        <BitChoiceGroup Label="Dynamic options" AutoReorderOptions @bind-Value="dynamicValue" TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string">
            <ItemPrefixTemplate Context="option">
                @(option.Index + 1).&nbsp;
            </ItemPrefixTemplate>
            <Options>
                @foreach (var (text, value) in dynamicOptions)
                {
                    <BitChoiceGroupOption @key="value" Text="@text" Value="@value" />
                }
            </Options>
        </BitChoiceGroup>
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Group description" RazorCode="@example16RazorCode" Id="example16">
    <div>Adds a helper text under the label to explain the whole set of choices, as opposed to the per-option Description that explains a single option. The group points its aria-describedby at it, so a screen reader announces it right after the name of the group. DescriptionTemplate replaces the plain text with custom content and takes precedence over Description when both are set.</div>
    <br />
    <div class="example-content column">
        <BitChoiceGroup Label="Deployment target"
                        Description="Only the selected environment receives the new build."
                        DefaultValue="@("Staging")"
                        TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string">
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Development" Value="@("Development")" />
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Staging" Value="@("Staging")" />
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Production" Value="@("Production")" />
        </BitChoiceGroup>

        <BitChoiceGroup Label="Deployment target" DefaultValue="@("Staging")" TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string">
            <DescriptionTemplate>
                <div class="custom-description">
                    <BitIcon IconName="@BitIconName.Info" />
                    <span>Only the selected environment receives the new build.</span>
                </div>
            </DescriptionTemplate>
            <Options>
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Development" Value="@("Development")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Staging" Value="@("Staging")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Production" Value="@("Production")" />
            </Options>
        </BitChoiceGroup>
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="FullWidth" RazorCode="@example17RazorCode" Id="example17" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>Stretches the ChoiceGroup to the full width of its container instead of letting it hug its widest option, which is what lines a ChoiceGroup up with the other full width fields of a form. In the horizontal layout the options additionally share that width in equal columns. In the vertical layout every option row spans the full width, so LabelPosition.Start now has room to push the options to the far edge; stretching the option label over that row as well turns it into the label-at-the-start, circle-at-the-end list found in settings pages.</div>
    <br />
    <div class="example-content column">
        <div class="example-box">
            <BitChoiceGroup Label="Default (hugs the widest option)" DefaultValue="@("A")" Horizontal TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string">
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option A" Value="@("A")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option B" Value="@("B")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option C" Value="@("C")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option D" Value="@("D")" />
            </BitChoiceGroup>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
            <BitChoiceGroup Label="FullWidth (horizontal, equal columns)" DefaultValue="@("A")" Horizontal FullWidth TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string">
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option A" Value="@("A")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option B" Value="@("B")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option C" Value="@("C")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option D" Value="@("D")" />
            </BitChoiceGroup>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
            <BitChoiceGroup Label="FullWidth + LabelPosition.Start (options at the far edge)" DefaultValue="@("A")" LabelPosition="BitLabelPosition.Start" FullWidth TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string">
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option A" Value="@("A")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option B" Value="@("B")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option C" Value="@("C")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option D" Value="@("D")" />
            </BitChoiceGroup>
        </div>
        <div class="example-box">
            <BitChoiceGroup Label="FullWidth + LabelPosition.Start + stretched option label (settings list)"
                            DefaultValue="@("A")"
                            LabelPosition="BitLabelPosition.Start" FullWidth
                            Styles="@(new() { ItemLabel = "width: 100%; justify-content: space-between;" })"
                            TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string">
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option A" Value="@("A")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option B" Value="@("B")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option C" Value="@("C")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option D" Value="@("D")" />
            </BitChoiceGroup>
        </div>
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="Item title" RazorCode="@example18RazorCode" Id="example18">
    <div>Sets the native browser tooltip of an option through its Title, which is handy when the option text is an abbreviation or gets clipped. Keep it supplementary: a title never appears on keyboard focus and is announced inconsistently by screen readers, so anything every user has to read belongs in the option text, its AriaLabel, or its Description.</div>
    <br />
    <div class="example-content">
        <BitChoiceGroup Label="Delivery window (hover an option)" DefaultValue="@("24h")" TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string">
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="1 h" Value="@("1h")" Title="Delivered within one hour of dispatch" />
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="24 h" Value="@("24h")" Title="Delivered within one business day" />
            <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="72 h" Value="@("72h")" Title="Delivered within three business days" />
        </BitChoiceGroup>
    </div>
</DemoExample>

<DemoExample Title="AutoFocus" RazorCode="@example19RazorCode" CsharpCode="@example19CsharpCode" Id="example19" PreventRenderForMcpClient>
    <div>Moves the keyboard focus into the ChoiceGroup on its first render. It lands on the checked option, or on the first enabled option when nothing is checked, which is exactly the option the Tab key would reach, and it is skipped entirely while the group is disabled or read-only. The button below mounts a fresh ChoiceGroup so the focus move stays visible on demand instead of stealing the focus as soon as this page loads.</div>
    <br />
    <div class="example-content column">
        <BitButton OnClick="() => showAutoFocus = !showAutoFocus">@(showAutoFocus ? "Unmount" : "Mount") the auto focused ChoiceGroup</BitButton>
        @if (showAutoFocus)
        {
            <BitChoiceGroup AutoFocus Label="Auto focused" DefaultValue="@("B")" TItem="BitChoiceGroupOption<string>" TValue="string">
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option A" Value="@("A")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option B" Value="@("B")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option C" Value="@("C")" />
                <BitChoiceGroupOption Text="Option D" Value="@("D")" />
            </BitChoiceGroup>
        }
    </div>
</DemoExample>

